import Native

{-
   What a container is, as classes - Implementation-Containers.md §5 and §17.

   `Length`, `Chunked` and `Contiguous`, the vector a chunk is read through, and the iteration
   protocol built on it. Separate from `Array.yana` because these are what a *user* type joins: the
   array is one container, and this is the interface every container has. The bulk operations
   written over the same protocol are `Reduce.yana`, and are not part of it - a container author
   writes none of them.
-}

{-
   The slice - Implementation-Containers.md §4.

   What a `[a]` parameter receives. `length` is declared over the *slice* and not over the owner,
   which is not an omission: reading is structural, so the operation that reads should ask for the
   borrow, and an owner reaches it by the ordinary conversion at the call - see convertSlice. §5's
   `Chunked` is this rule generalized to containers that are not contiguous.

   Reading an element lives one layer down, in `Native`'s `instance Index(Flat(a), Size, a)`, for
   the same reason and by the same conversion. Growth is not here at all - it is nominal, and `push`
   says `Array(a)`.
-}
{-
   How many elements a container holds, as a class - Implementation-Containers.md §17's reasoning
   applied to the other universal operation.

   `length` was a plain function over `Flat(a)`, which served every container by the ordinary
   owner-to-slice conversion at the call. `String` cannot reach it that way: it is not a container of
   elements with a buffer address, so there is no slice for it to convert to, and its length lives in
   a different place on each target. A second plain `fn length` is a duplicate declaration rather
   than an overload, so the name has to become a class for the two to coexist - which is exactly what
   happened to `xs[i]` when `Index` landed, and for the same reason.

   The container instances are the same set `Index` has and are there for the same reason: instance
   selection does not convert, so an `Array(a)` binds `Array(a)` and needs an instance of its own
   rather than reaching the slice's through the conversion a *call* would have performed.

   **Generated rather than written, on the same terms `Index`'s are - see the note in `Native`.**
   Written out they would read:

       instance Length('[a]):
           fn length(self: [a]) -> Size = self.length

       @platform(native) instance Length(Array(a)):
           fn length(self: Array(a)) -> Size = self.length :: Size

       @platform(js) instance Length(Array(a)):
           fn length(self: Array(a)) -> Size = hostLength(self.items)

   One field load each. `String`'s instances below are ordinary functions and stay in source,
   because that argument is not about them.
-}
pub class Length(c):
  fn length(self: c) -> Size

{-
   `Chunked` and `Contiguous` - Implementation-Containers.md §5.

   Contiguity is the one-chunk case of a more general property, and both are wanted. `Chunked` is
   what a function that only *reads elements* should ask for: it accepts every container, and after
   specialization there is no dispatch, because the push-style iterator lowering means a contiguous
   container yields one slice and fuses into the loop it compiles to today while a chunked one
   becomes a nested loop whose inner loop runs at full contiguous speed. `Contiguous` is a promise
   about a buffer address - one load to index, passable to a syscall, `memcpy`-able - which is what
   `[a]` means, and the conversion that lets a container of your own be passed as one lives in
   `convert`.

   The dependency is what makes either usable: the container decides the element type, so `for x in
   chunks(xs)` and `fn (Chunked(c, a)) count(xs: c)` both bind `c` from what the caller wrote and
   read `a` back off the instance. `Widen`'s entry in classes.md says why that is declared rather
   than inferred.

   `chunks` hands over a *view*, written `'[a]` because a class member has no binding to carry the
   capability - the position rule's spelling for a shared window in a type position - and `&[a]`
   where one is written through. What a chunk is is a window into the container's storage, so a
   mutable one writes through and neither ever copies. The superclass is what stops a
   container from supplying half of this - a `Contiguous` instance with no `Chunked` one is rejected
   where it is declared, and its `Chunked` instance is the one line below.

   There is deliberately no implicit flattening: satisfying `Contiguous` from chunks costs an O(n)
   allocation, and hiding one behind an argument convention is the performance surprise Design.md
   rules out. A `flatten` returning an owner is fine; a coercion is not, which is why a container
   that is `Chunked` and not `Contiguous` is rejected where `[a]` is expected with a diagnostic that
   names `Chunked` as what to ask for instead.
-}
pub class Chunked(c -> a):
  iter fn chunks(self: c) -> '[a]

pub class (Chunked(c, a)) Contiguous(c -> a):
  fn elements(return self: c) -> '[a]

{-
   And the writable half, which is a second promise rather than a second spelling of the first.

   `Contiguous` says this container has a buffer address, and `convert` spends that at an argument
   position: a container of the program's own reaches `fn f(xs: [a])` through one call to
   `elements`, which is the only implicit conversion into a slice a program can grant itself. What
   it did *not* reach was `fn f(&xs: [a])`, and the reason was not an oversight - `elements` hands
   back what its signature says, a **shared** view, and a conversion may not widen one. That is the
   rule Analysis-Borrows.md §2.3 removed `applyReturnRootMutability` for, and an argument position
   is exactly where it would have come back.

   So the write is a member of its own, on a class of its own, and both halves of that matter:

   - **A member of its own** so that the exclusive window is the *instance's* promise. `elementsMut`
     is declared `&self: 'c` and returns `&[a]`, so the caller's container is exclusively
     borrowed for as long as the window lives and the loan is rooted in it by the class's group.
     Nothing is promoted anywhere.
   - **A class of its own** so that a container may promise one and not the other, which §7.1 makes
     the same argument for with `Index.get` and `Index.getMut`: a type with stable read-only storage
     can hand out an address to read and has no business promising one to write. A container that
     implements only `Contiguous` is passable where `[a]` is read and refused where one is written,
     with a diagnostic that names this class.

   `&[a]` in the result is what made this declarable at all, and it is new: until §4.5 gave the
   slice descriptor's type somewhere to carry its capability, `-> &[a]` *was* `-> '[a]` and
   the class could not say what it wanted. The `Array` and `[a *n]` instances are one word each -
   the conversion at the return position builds the exclusive descriptor over the exclusive borrow
   `&self` already is - and `Flat(a)`'s is the identity, since a window already is one.
-}
pub class (Contiguous(c, a)) Writable(c -> a):
  fn elementsMut(&self: 'c) -> &[a]

{-
   The two instances the library itself supplies, which is §5's "the standard library adopts it in
   the same change": every container that exists is one of these, so a signature written against
   `Chunked` accepts everything a `[a]` one does.

   `elements` on the owner goes through a *call* rather than building the descriptor by hand, and the
   reason is ownership rather than brevity: the conversion at that call is what records the result as
   a view of `self` (Local::viewOf), which is what makes the returned borrow rooted in the argument
   the signature marked `return`. A hand-built `Flat` would be rooted in nothing and would type-check
   for the wrong reason.

   Which call it is is `unclampedSlice` rather than `slice`, and that is a size decision - see the
   note there. The two are the same conversion at the same argument position, so the rooting is
   unchanged; what goes is three clamps that a whole container can never trip.

   On the slice itself `elements` is the identity, because a `Flat(a)` already is the descriptor.
-}
@platform(native) instance Contiguous(Array(a), a):
    fn elements(return self: Array(a)) -> '[a] = unclampedSlice(self, 0, self.length :: Size)

@platform(js) instance Contiguous(Array(a), a):
    fn elements(return self: Array(a)) -> '[a] = unclampedSlice(self, 0, self.length :: Size)

instance Chunked(Array(a), a):
    iter fn chunks(self: Array(a)) -> '[a] = yield elements(self)

instance Contiguous('[a], a):
    fn elements(self: '[a]) -> '[a] = self

instance Writable(Array(a), a):
    fn elementsMut(&self: Array(a)) -> &[a] = self

instance Writable('[a], a):
    fn elementsMut(&self: [a]) -> &[a] = self

instance Chunked('[a], a):
    iter fn chunks(self: [a]) -> '[a] = yield elements(self)

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   When two containers are equal
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   The same length and the same elements in the same order, which is the only definition a sequence
   has - and it is one body over `Reduce.yana`'s `itemsEqual`, so a container of a type with a vector
   on this target compares a register of elements a turn and one of anything else compares them one
   at a time. Neither instance says which; that is `itemsEqual`'s to decide and it decides it from
   the element.

   **The length test is first and it is what makes the comparison's bound safe**, exactly as it is on
   `String`: `itemsEqual` compares `left`'s length in both windows and has no answer for two windows
   of different sizes.

   Three heads and not one, because there is no class that would let one serve. An `Array(a)`, a
   `Flat(a)` and an `[a *n]` are three types and instance selection does not convert, so a comparison
   of two arrays cannot go through the slice's instance the way `xs[i]` goes through the slice's
   `Index` - which is `Array`'s own note on why its subscript is a second instance rather than a
   conversion. The bodies are the same line three times and the alternative is a `Contiguous(c, a)`
   blanket instance, which would claim `Eq` for every container anyone ever writes.

   `Ord` is deliberately absent. Lexicographic order over a container is a decision about what a
   *shorter* container means and about whether the order is the elements' or something the container
   states, and nothing in the library asks for one yet.
-}
instance (Eq(a)) Eq(Array(a)):
    fn ==(self: Array(a), other: Array(a)) -> Bool:
        if length(self) != length(other) then return False
        return itemsEqual(elements(self), elements(other))

instance (Eq(a)) Eq('[a]):
    fn ==(self: [a], other: [a]) -> Bool:
        if length(self) != length(other) then return False
        return itemsEqual(elements(self), elements(other))

instance (n: Int, Eq(a)) Eq([a *n]):
    fn ==(self: [a *n], other: [a *n]) -> Bool = itemsEqual(elements(self), elements(other))

{-
   Every element of a container, one at a time - the protocol's other half.

   `chunks` is what a container *implements*, because a run of elements is what it can hand over
   without promising anything about the next one. This is what a caller wants, and until it existed
   every caller wrote the same two nested loops: a `for` over the chunks and a `while` over an index
   inside it, which is four lines and an index variable to say "each element".

   **It costs nothing.** The optimizer flattens the continuation into the caller and then folds the
   chunk loop away for a container that has one chunk, so `for v in items(xs)` and the hand-written
   index loop reach the same instructions - the same `mul`, `add`, load, accumulate and increment,
   in the same blocks. That is measured rather than assumed; the two were compared in the optimized
   IR before this was written.

   Yields `'a` and not `a`, which is what makes it available to an element type that owns something:
   a borrow of the element is a view into the container, and a caller that wants a value of its own
   asks `copy` for one. `Reduce.yana` reads through it, `Join.yana` copies out of it, and neither
   has to say which the protocol supports.
-}
pub iter fn (Chunked(c, a)) items(self: c) -> 'a:
    for chunk in chunks(self):
        let n = length(chunk)
        let &i = 0 :: Size

        while i < n:
            yield chunk[i]
            i = i + 1

{-
   And the fixed array, which had no `length` at all.

   `[a *n]` already reaches every `[a]` function through the conversion at a call - that is
   Implementation-Containers.md §6, and it is what makes `total(xs)` in the fixed-array fixture
   compile with no instance anywhere. What it could not do is answer `length` about *itself*, which
   a generic body constrained on `Length(c)` asks, and the answer costs nothing: a fixed array stores
   no count, so what every other container here loads is a constant.

   `(n: Int)` is the context and `a` is introduced by the head, which is the instance form const
   generics landed with - see `Sized` in test/resolve/ConstGeneric.yana.

   **`Chunked` and `Contiguous` beside it were absent, and the reason has since gone away.** The
   note that stood here said a compiler limitation rather than a decision about containers:
   `Chunked`'s member is an `iter fn`, and a *parametric* instance of one - which this is, the count
   being a variable - could not have its continuation result inferred when the body that selects it
   was compiled with specialization declined, so the pair compiled in a specialized build and failed
   in the erased one with "cannot infer type argument $r of Chunked([a *n], a).chunks".

   Re-tested rather than repeated, which is the only way that claim was ever going to stop being
   true: `test/resolve/FixedContainer.yana` runs both forms and both answer the same number. What it
   buys is every function written against a container rather than against an array - `items`, the
   folds in `Reduce.yana`, `concat` and `joinWith` in `Join.yana`, and `sort`, which `Sort.yana`
   singled out as the one thing a fixed array could not reach.

   Nothing changes for `Digest`, whose result type wanted these and names the *width* in the class
   head instead - `Digest(d -> n: Int)`, so `finish` answers `[U8 *n]` and a generic body has both
   the bytes, by the conversion at the call, and the count, as a value. That is the better signature
   independently of this.
-}
instance (n: Int) Length([a *n]):
    fn length(self: [a *n]) -> Size = n :: Size

instance (n: Int) Contiguous([a *n], a):
    fn elements(return self: [a *n]) -> '[a] = unclampedSlice(self, 0, n :: Size)

instance (n: Int) Chunked([a *n], a):
    iter fn chunks(self: [a *n]) -> '[a] = yield elements(self)

instance (n: Int) Writable([a *n], a):
    fn elementsMut(&self: [a *n]) -> &[a] = self

{-
   Reading a whole vector out of a chunk, and writing one back - Implementation-Vector.md §12's "a
   way to read a vector out of a `Flat(a)`", which is what the iteration protocol below is built on.

   Core's portable set deliberately has no load in it: `splat`, `lane` and the rearrangements are
   operations on a value, and where a value comes *from* is a question about storage. So these three
   are the crossing, and they are here rather than in `Native` because the type they take is what
   makes them safe: a `Flat(a)` is a window into storage the language allocated, and §5.2 of
   Design-Vector is what says such storage is padded. A raw address promises nothing, which is why
   `Native.vectorPast` takes one and this does not.

   Two implementations and one signature, which is the split every container operation here already
   has. Natively a vector transfer is one instruction at an address; on JS it is `lanes` element
   accesses, because a vector on that target *is* `lanes` values (Design-Vector §7.2).

   `loadVectorTail` is the one place the two targets differ in what they *mean* rather than in how
   they spell it. Natively it overreads - it loads a whole vector from `at`, reading up to a vector's
   width past the end of the object, which §8's guarantee says is safe and which is the whole reason
   that guarantee exists. On JS there is no such guarantee to have and a read past the end of a host
   array is `undefined` rather than an unspecified byte, so the lanes past the end repeat the last
   element instead. Both are values the caller is about to mask off; neither is a number that could
   poison an accumulator before it does.
-}
@platform(native) pub fn loadVector(from: [a], at: Size) -> Vec(a) = vectorAt(from.items + at)
@platform(js) pub fn loadVector(from: [a], at: Size) -> Vec(a) = hostVector(from.items, from.offset + at)

@platform(native) pub fn loadVectorTail(from: [a], at: Size) -> Vec(a) = vectorPast(from.items + at)
@platform(js) pub fn loadVectorTail(from: [a], at: Size) -> Vec(a) =
    hostVectorUpTo(from.items, from.offset + at, from.offset + from.length - 1)

@platform(native) pub fn storeVector(to: [a], at: Size, value: Vec(a)) -> {} = setVectorAt(to.items + at, value)
@platform(js) pub fn storeVector(to: [a], at: Size, value: Vec(a)) -> {} =
    hostSetVector(to.items, to.offset + at, value)

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The machine words of a binary format.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   Reading and writing a 16-, 32- or 64-bit word at a stated byte order, `at` bytes into a byte
   slice.
   Every format that is not text is made of these - a digest, a decoder, a wire protocol, a file
   header - and doing it a word at a time rather than a byte at a time is most of what makes any of
   them fast.

   Here rather than beside the one library that first needed them, and as compiler intrinsics rather
   than as source: what the operation costs is a target question at both ends, and neither target's
   answer is expressible in portable source.

   **Natively it is one load and one `bswap`**, or one `movbe` at v3 - see `selectByteSwapMemory`.
   The load is unaligned, which x86 has always done and which is why these take a `[U8]` rather than
   demanding an aligned buffer. The order costs nothing where the target already agrees, because the
   comparison in `bigEndian` folds against `Target.byteOrder`.

   **On JS it is one `DataView` accessor**, through a view built once per array - see
   `NativeOp::HostWordRead`, where the measurements are. At 16 and 32 bits that matches the shift
   chain it replaces; at 64 bits it is 2x on a read and 7x on a write, which is the difference between
   a `U64` being assembled out of bytes in the `bigint` domain and being read in one call.

   **16 bits is included because nothing excludes it**, which is worth saying because the operation
   underneath is the one width where the two targets differ in shape: `bswap r16` is undefined on x86
   and `movbe` has no 16-bit producer, so `lowerCalc` spends a 16-bit reversal as the shift pair
   rather than as an instruction. That is still one load and two ALU operations against two loads, a
   shift and an or - and every wire format with a length prefix or a port number in it wants this.

   The writers are worth as much as the readers and for a plainer reason: written as four subscript
   assignments a writer is a 46-instruction function that the inliner *declines*, and a `finish`
   calls one eight times per digest. One store is one instruction and there is nothing left to
   decline.
-}
{-
   The one bounds test all twelve share - `at` names a *window* of `width` bytes rather than one
   element, so `checkIndexInBounds`' single compare is not the question being asked here.

   Written as the mistake, which is what `checkCondition` takes, and read in the unsigned domain for
   `checkIndexInBounds`' reason: a negative `at` is above every length there is when the bits are
   read that way, so one compare covers both ends. The `length < width` test in front of it is what
   keeps `length - width` from underflowing into a bound that admits everything - `||` is lazy, so
   the subtraction happens only where it has an answer.

   It costs nothing where the caller's index is provable: `opt_check` removes a check whose condition
   is known, which is every read at a literal offset into a block whose length the loop above already
   established. Where it is not provable it is one compare and a predicted-not-taken branch, which is
   what a subscript costs, and these exist to be used where a subscript would otherwise be.
-}
fn checkWordInBounds(from: [U8], at: Size, width: Size) -> {}:
    let count = length(from)
    checkCondition(count < width || (bitcast(at) :: USize) > (bitcast(count - width) :: USize))
    return

@platform(native) pub fn readBigU16(from: [U8], at: Size) -> U16:
    checkWordInBounds(from, at, 2)
    return bigEndian(*(bitcast(from.items + at) :: %U16))

@platform(js) pub fn readBigU16(from: [U8], at: Size) -> U16:
    checkWordInBounds(from, at, 2)
    return hostReadU16(from.items, from.offset + at, False)

@platform(native) pub fn readLittleU16(from: [U8], at: Size) -> U16:
    checkWordInBounds(from, at, 2)
    return littleEndian(*(bitcast(from.items + at) :: %U16))

@platform(js) pub fn readLittleU16(from: [U8], at: Size) -> U16:
    checkWordInBounds(from, at, 2)
    return hostReadU16(from.items, from.offset + at, True)

@platform(native) pub fn readBigU32(from: [U8], at: Size) -> U32:
    checkWordInBounds(from, at, 4)
    return bigEndian(*(bitcast(from.items + at) :: %U32))

@platform(js) pub fn readBigU32(from: [U8], at: Size) -> U32:
    checkWordInBounds(from, at, 4)
    return hostReadU32(from.items, from.offset + at, False)

@platform(native) pub fn readLittleU32(from: [U8], at: Size) -> U32:
    checkWordInBounds(from, at, 4)
    return littleEndian(*(bitcast(from.items + at) :: %U32))

@platform(js) pub fn readLittleU32(from: [U8], at: Size) -> U32:
    checkWordInBounds(from, at, 4)
    return hostReadU32(from.items, from.offset + at, True)

@platform(native) pub fn readBigU64(from: [U8], at: Size) -> U64:
    checkWordInBounds(from, at, 8)
    return bigEndian(*(bitcast(from.items + at) :: %U64))

@platform(js) pub fn readBigU64(from: [U8], at: Size) -> U64:
    checkWordInBounds(from, at, 8)
    return hostReadU64(from.items, from.offset + at, False)

@platform(native) pub fn readLittleU64(from: [U8], at: Size) -> U64:
    checkWordInBounds(from, at, 8)
    return littleEndian(*(bitcast(from.items + at) :: %U64))

@platform(js) pub fn readLittleU64(from: [U8], at: Size) -> U64:
    checkWordInBounds(from, at, 8)
    return hostReadU64(from.items, from.offset + at, True)

@platform(native) pub fn writeBigU16(&to: [U8], at: Size, value: U16) -> {}:
    checkWordInBounds(to, at, 2)
    store(bitcast(to.items + at) :: %U16, bigEndian(value))

@platform(js) pub fn writeBigU16(&to: [U8], at: Size, value: U16) -> {}:
    checkWordInBounds(to, at, 2)
    hostWriteU16(to.items, to.offset + at, value, False)

@platform(native) pub fn writeLittleU16(&to: [U8], at: Size, value: U16) -> {}:
    checkWordInBounds(to, at, 2)
    store(bitcast(to.items + at) :: %U16, littleEndian(value))

@platform(js) pub fn writeLittleU16(&to: [U8], at: Size, value: U16) -> {}:
    checkWordInBounds(to, at, 2)
    hostWriteU16(to.items, to.offset + at, value, True)

@platform(native) pub fn writeBigU32(&to: [U8], at: Size, value: U32) -> {}:
    checkWordInBounds(to, at, 4)
    store(bitcast(to.items + at) :: %U32, bigEndian(value))

@platform(js) pub fn writeBigU32(&to: [U8], at: Size, value: U32) -> {}:
    checkWordInBounds(to, at, 4)
    hostWriteU32(to.items, to.offset + at, value, False)

@platform(native) pub fn writeLittleU32(&to: [U8], at: Size, value: U32) -> {}:
    checkWordInBounds(to, at, 4)
    store(bitcast(to.items + at) :: %U32, littleEndian(value))

@platform(js) pub fn writeLittleU32(&to: [U8], at: Size, value: U32) -> {}:
    checkWordInBounds(to, at, 4)
    hostWriteU32(to.items, to.offset + at, value, True)

@platform(native) pub fn writeBigU64(&to: [U8], at: Size, value: U64) -> {}:
    checkWordInBounds(to, at, 8)
    store(bitcast(to.items + at) :: %U64, bigEndian(value))

@platform(js) pub fn writeBigU64(&to: [U8], at: Size, value: U64) -> {}:
    checkWordInBounds(to, at, 8)
    hostWriteU64(to.items, to.offset + at, value, False)

@platform(native) pub fn writeLittleU64(&to: [U8], at: Size, value: U64) -> {}:
    checkWordInBounds(to, at, 8)
    store(bitcast(to.items + at) :: %U64, littleEndian(value))

@platform(js) pub fn writeLittleU64(&to: [U8], at: Size, value: U64) -> {}:
    checkWordInBounds(to, at, 8)
    hostWriteU64(to.items, to.offset + at, value, True)

{-
   The iteration protocol - Design-Vector §4.1, Implementation-Vector.md §9 item 5.

   This is what the whole vector design is for: a loop written over `vectors(xs)` is one loop, it
   reads every element of the container exactly once, and the last iteration over each chunk is the
   same body as the others rather than a scalar epilogue somebody had to remember to write.

   Over `Chunked` and not `Contiguous`, per Design-Vector §4.1: a contiguous container yields one
   chunk and the outer loop folds away, so the general formulation costs the common case nothing,
   while a deque or a rope reaches full speed inside each of its runs.

   **`vectors` forces the identity into the lanes past the end.** It is not `vectorsMasked` with the
   mask thrown away, and this is a ruling rather than a convenience - §4.4 of the design says a body
   handles the padding lanes "by selecting the identity into the masked-off lanes before
   accumulating", which the *body* cannot do, because it is the mask that says which lanes those are
   and this is the iterator that does not hand one over. Zero is the identity for `+`, `-`, `|` and
   `^`; a body accumulating with `*`, `min` or `max` writes `vectorsMasked` and forces its own. The
   cost is one `select` per chunk tail and nothing in the full-width iterations, where the mask is
   all-set and the select folds to its own operand.

   The live count travels as an `Int` rather than as the `Mask(a)` it stands for, which is a choice
   and not a limitation - a mask may be held in memory since §9.9. One integer against sixteen to
   sixty-four bytes, `maskUpTo(live)` folds to all-set on every full-width iteration, and §4.4 wants
   the tail mask read out of a table indexed by exactly this number.

   **The chunk index is a `Size` and the test is against `n - width`, and those two lines are worth
   more than everything else in this iterator put together** - §58.5 of test/bench/findings.md.
   Written the obvious way, `let &i = 0 :: Int` with `while i + width <= n`, this loop costs two
   instructions that have nothing to do with the work:

     - `i` is an `Int`, so every `loadVector(chunk, i :: Size)` sign-extends it into the address and
       the loop carries a `movslq` it did not ask for. `widenInductionVariables` exists for exactly
       this and cannot take it: its no-wrap proofs want a strict test with a step of one, and a chunk
       walk has an inclusive test with a step of four, eight or sixteen.
     - `i + width <= n` recomputes the *next* index to test it, so the loop carries a second `add`
       (emitted as a `lea`) beside the one that steps. Hoisting `n - width` out makes the test read
       the index the step already produced.

   Measured on `maximum` over `[Int]` at v2, with each loop transcribed out of the linked binaries
   into `test/bench/chunkloop.s`: 1.66x against `llc -Os` as emitted, 1.39x with the `lea` gone,
   1.34x with the index widened, and **0.96x with both**. Neither alone is worth much and together
   they are worth all of it - which is the reason this reads the way it does rather than the way it
   would be written.

   `Size` is `I64` natively and `Int` on JS, so `n - width` is a signed subtraction on both and goes
   negative rather than wrapping when a chunk is shorter than one vector - which is the case the test
   has to reject, and does. It is the same guard `i + width <= n` made at `i = 0`.

   Both targets. This said "native only for now, and the reason is not vectors: an `iter fn` that
   yields from inside a `for` captures the continuation it was handed, and a reference to a function
   value the JS backend flattened into two variables has no object to name". Whatever was true of
   that once, it is not now - this iterator and `items` above both yield from inside a `for`, and
   both were run on the JavaScript build before the claim was removed.
-}
pub iter fn (Chunked(c, a)) vectorsMasked(self: c) -> {Vec(a), Int}:
    let width = lanes(zero() :: Vec(a)) :: Size

    for chunk in chunks(self):
        let n = length(chunk)
        let last = n - width
        let &i = 0 :: Size

        while i <= last:
            yield {loadVector(chunk, i), truncate(width) :: Int}
            i = i + width

        if i < n:
            yield {loadVectorTail(chunk, i), truncate(n - i) :: Int}

pub iter fn (Chunked(c, a)) vectors(self: c) -> Vec(a):
    let empty = zero() :: Vec(a)
    let width = lanes(empty) :: Size

    for chunk in chunks(self):
        let n = length(chunk)
        let last = n - width
        let &i = 0 :: Size

        while i <= last:
            yield loadVector(chunk, i)
            i = i + width

        if i < n:
            let live = maskUpTo(truncate(n - i) :: Int) :: Mask(a)
            yield select(live, loadVectorTail(chunk, i), empty)
